  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 445 AM EDT MON SEP 14 2026\n\nThe extended portion of the forecast remains on track with the\nidea of a large 5h ridge holding over the region through much of\nthe week before some weakening and drifting of its center is \nprogged towards next weekend. This could perhaps include the Gulf\nbecoming more active late in the period - not necessarily a\nrecipe for helping to clear the local ridge. The net result will \ncontinue to mean temperatures staying unseasonable warm and\nseriously limiting the potential for widespread rain.\n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nThe extended period opens with an upper-level ridge settling into\nthe Ohio and Tennessee valleys and surrounding areas of the\nSoutheast US. Elsewhere, a trough resides north of the Northern\nPlains. Models depict a 598-600 dam high in the vicinity of \nTennessee Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nBy Thursday, the aforementioned trough attempts to dig southeast into\nthe Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. However, model-to-model runs of\nthe LREF Grand Ensemble show a stronger ridge, with influence\nextending further north into the Ohio Valley compared to previous\nruns. The upper-level ridge does eventually weaken and retrograde\nback to the southwest toward Texas heading into the weekend. This\nwill leave the area under zonal flow with weak troughing.\n\nAt the surface, conditions remain fair both Tuesday and Wednesday,\nwith highs in the upper 80s to low 90s. A weak warm front will \npass through the forecast area from the southwest on Tuesday. \nWednesday and Thursday, a surface cold front extending southwest \nfrom Quebec through the Ohio Valley, approaches the forecast area\nfrom the northwest. The stronger ridge mentioned previously has \ntrended the model mean temperatures warmer for Thursday and has \nlowered forecaster confidence on the front actually coming \nthrough. 850-mb temperatures around 19-20 C suggest surface \ntemperatures could climb into the upper 80s to lower 90s on \nThursday. At JKL, the record high temperature is currently 90, set\nback in 1991. This could be in play. One thing that may hinder \nthose chances would be any added cloud cover or convection. \nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible \nthough confidence is low. Model spread does increase Thursday \nonward surrounding the evolution of the nearby front. Therefore \nshower and storm chances remain in the forecast through Sunday. \nForecast temperatures should cool some heading into the weekend, \nwith highs in the 80s.",
